  putting a stealable credential in a place JavaScript can read. It also keeps this design honest about the fact that
  the backend contract is still assumed.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What does the persisted client-side identity contain? → Identifier only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: how the shared layer is actually delivered. This is a real fork because of how this repo is wired — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m
  loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a plain classic script, while [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m for Node. Neither gives the browser a module
  system, so "readable across the app by other forms later" needs one picked:

  - [1mES modules[0m — switch [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m and use real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m for an identity
    module. Native, no build step, and it's the direction the platform settled on. It changes the script's loading
    semantics (deferred, module scope, and it won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m — it needs a local server), so it's a small but
    real behavior change to the existing page.
  - [1mGlobal on [38;5;153mwindow[0m — a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m defines [38;5;153mwindow.Identity[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m runs. Zero change to how anything loads
    and it works from [38;5;153mfile://[39m. But load order becomes an invisible dependency, and a global is the thing you'd have to
    unpick later when there are several consumers.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — the most conventional answer for a real app, and the most machinery: a build step, a dependency, and
    a [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m that currently has no scripts and no dependencies at all.

  I recommend [1mES modules[0m: it gives you a genuine shared module for the future forms without adding a build system to a
  repo that has none, and the cost is one attribute plus serving over http instead of opening the file directly.
